@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@section Overview

Selected Window Accent Mode is a global minor mode that helps you keep
track of which window has focus by visually accenting it.  It applies
distinctive colors to the fringes, mode line, and header line of the
selected window.

In the @code{tiling} style the selected window is surrounded by a
complete outline: accent-colored fringes on the left and right, an
accented header line across the top, and an accented mode line across
the bottom.

The mode uses the same fringe widths on every window, so moving focus
does not change a window's text area and does not shift its contents.
It does not modify window margins, which leaves packages such as
@code{olivetti-mode} and @code{visual-fill-column} free to use them.

This is particularly helpful when working with complex window layouts or
themes where the distinction between active and inactive windows is
subtle.

@section Features

@itemize @bullet
@item Visually highlights the currently selected window
@item Multiple accent styles: @code{default}, @code{tiling}, and @code{subtle}
@item Full four-sided outline in @code{tiling} style
@item Stable layout: all windows reserve the same fringe widths, so
contents do not shift when focus moves
@item Customizable accent colors from the theme highlight, a custom
color, or a Pywal palette
@item Optional tiling header line, preserving any header lines that
windows already display
@item Optional tab-bar integration
@item Smart borders option for single-window frames
@end itemize

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

Selected Window Accent Mode requires Emacs 29.1 or later.

@section Package Repositories

The easiest way to install Selected Window Accent Mode is through MELPA.

@example
M-x package-install RET selected-window-accent-mode RET
@end example

@section Manual Installation

You can also install manually by downloading the package from GitHub:
@url{https://github.com/captainflasmr/selected-window-accent-mode}

@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration

@section Basic Configuration

Here's a simple configuration using @code{use-package}:

@example
(use-package selected-window-accent-mode
  :config (selected-window-accent-mode 1)
  :custom
  (selected-window-accent-fringe-thickness 10)
  (selected-window-accent-custom-color nil)
  (selected-window-accent-mode-style 'subtle))
@end example

This enables the mode with the @code{subtle} style, using the current
theme's highlight color for accents and setting the fringe thickness to
10 pixels.

@section Using a Custom Color

If you want to specify your own accent color:

@example
(use-package selected-window-accent-mode
  :config (selected-window-accent-mode 1)
  :custom
  (selected-window-accent-fringe-thickness 10)
  (selected-window-accent-custom-color "orange")
  (selected-window-accent-mode-style 'tiling)
  (selected-window-accent-percentage-darken 0)
  (selected-window-accent-percentage-desaturate 0)
  (selected-window-accent-tab-accent t)
  (selected-window-accent-smart-borders t))
@end example

A custom color takes precedence over the theme highlight color, but a
Pywal color takes precedence over both when
@code{selected-window-accent-use-pywal} is non-nil and the selected
Pywal palette color is available.

@section Tweaking Theme Highlight Color

You can also use the theme's highlight color but adjust it:

@example
(use-package selected-window-accent-mode
  :config (selected-window-accent-mode 1)
  :custom
  (selected-window-accent-fringe-thickness 10)
  (selected-window-accent-custom-color nil)
  (selected-window-accent-mode-style 'tiling)
  (selected-window-accent-percentage-darken 20)
  (selected-window-accent-percentage-desaturate 20)
  (selected-window-accent-tab-accent t)
  (selected-window-accent-smart-borders t))
@end example

The @code{selected-window-accent-percentage-darken} and
@code{selected-window-accent-percentage-desaturate} options apply when
no custom or Pywal color is in use.

@section Using Pywal

When @code{selected-window-accent-use-pywal} is non-nil and the Pywal
palette file @file{~/.cache/wal/colors.json} provides the selected
color, that color is used for the accent.  Use
@code{selected-window-accent-pywal-color} to choose the palette entry;
it defaults to @code{"color1"} and accepts entries from @code{color0}
through @code{color15}.

@example
(use-package selected-window-accent-mode
  :config (selected-window-accent-mode 1)
  :custom
  (selected-window-accent-fringe-thickness 10)
  (selected-window-accent-mode-style 'tiling)
  (selected-window-accent-use-pywal t)
  (selected-window-accent-pywal-color "color5"))
@end example

If the palette file is missing, the mode falls back to the custom color
or the theme highlight color.  If you choose a custom accent color while
Pywal is enabled and its palette file exists, the custom color is
ignored and a short message is shown in the minibuffer.  Toggle Pywal
off with the @kbd{p} key in the command map, or with
@code{M-x selected-window-accent-toggle-pywal RET}.

@section Tiling Outline

In @code{tiling} style the mode reserves a header line on every window
so that the selected window has an accent on all four sides.  Windows
that already display a header line keep their content; windows without
one get a blank header line.

The header line height follows
@code{selected-window-accent-fringe-thickness}, converted using the
frame's font metrics so the top border matches the fringe thickness in
pixels.  The reserved header line reduces each window's text area by its
height.

Set @code{selected-window-accent-tiling-outline} to @code{nil} to
disable the top header line and keep the previous behavior.

@section Keybinding Setup

To set up a key binding for the command map:

@example
(global-set-key (kbd "C-c w") selected-window-accent-map)
@end example

@node Usage
@chapter Usage

@section Basic Usage

Once enabled, the mode automatically highlights the selected window.
No further actions are required for basic functionality.

The mode installs functions on
@code{window-configuration-change-hook} and
@code{window-state-change-hook}, so the accent follows window selection,
window creation and deletion, and buffer changes.

@section Switching Styles

You can interactively switch between different accent styles using:

@example
M-x selected-window-accent-switch-style RET
@end example

You'll be prompted to choose between:

@itemize @bullet
@item @code{default} --- standard fringe appearance (fringes use
@code{selected-window-accent-fringe-minimum}), with the selected
window's mode line still accented.
@item @code{tiling} --- a full outline: thick accent fringes on the left
and right, an accented header line on top, and an accented mode line on
the bottom.  The border heights follow
@code{selected-window-accent-fringe-thickness}.
@item @code{subtle} --- a narrower accent: the left fringe and the mode
line only.
@end itemize

In @code{tiling} and @code{subtle} styles, every window reserves the
same fringe widths, and only the selected window's fringes are colored.
This keeps buffer contents from shifting when focus moves between
windows.

Tiling style also reserves a header line on every window so the selected
window is outlined on all four sides.  Existing header lines are
preserved; a blank header line is added where needed.  Set
@code{selected-window-accent-tiling-outline} to @code{nil} to disable
the top header line.

@section Changing Color

To interactively change the accent color:

@example
M-x selected-window-accent-switch-color RET
@end example

With the command map bound, @kbd{RET} and @kbd{j} both run this command.
If Pywal is enabled and its palette file exists, the selected custom
color is ignored and a short message is shown in the minibuffer.  Toggle
Pywal off before choosing a custom color.

@section Toggling Pywal

To toggle Pywal colors:

@example
M-x selected-window-accent-toggle-pywal RET
@end example

With the command map bound, @kbd{p} toggles Pywal colors.  When Pywal is
enabled and its palette file exists, the accent color comes from the
palette instead of the custom color or theme highlight.

@section Using the Command Map

If you've set up the keybinding for the command map (e.g., @kbd{C-c w}),
you can access the following commands:

@table @kbd
@item C-c w w
Switch accent style
@item C-c w n
Switch accent style
@item C-c w RET
Change accent color
@item C-c w j
Change accent color
@item C-c w t
Toggle tab bar accenting
@item C-c w l
Synchronize tab bar with theme
@item C-c w b
Toggle smart borders
@item C-c w p
Toggle Pywal colors
@end table

@node Command Reference
@chapter Command Reference

@table @code
@item selected-window-accent-mode
Toggle the global minor mode on or off.  When enabled, the selected
window is visually accented.

@item selected-window-accent-switch-style
Switch between the @code{default}, @code{tiling}, and @code{subtle}
accent styles and apply the new style immediately.

@item selected-window-accent-switch-color
Interactively choose a new custom accent color and apply it.

@item selected-window-accent-toggle-tab-accent
Toggle whether the accent color is also applied to the active tab in
the tab bar.

@item selected-window-accent-toggle-smart-borders
Toggle smart borders.  When enabled, fringe, header-line, and mode-line
accenting is disabled for frames that contain only one window.

@item selected-window-accent-toggle-pywal
Toggle the use of a color from the Pywal palette.

@item selected-window-accent-sync-tab-bar-to-theme
Synchronize the tab bar faces with the current theme, removing any
custom accenting applied to the tab bar.
@end table

@node Customization
@chapter Customization

@section Available Options

@table @code
@item selected-window-accent-fringe-thickness
The thickness of the accent fringes in pixels.  Default is 6.  In
@code{tiling} style this value also determines the header line and
mode-line border heights.

@item selected-window-accent-fringe-minimum
Minimum fringe width in pixels to preserve in @code{default} style.
When set to a positive value (e.g., 8), fringes remain visible in
@code{default} style so that fringe indicators such as line-wrapping
arrows, git-gutter marks, and flycheck icons are usable.  The fringe
background matches the buffer background so indicators blend
naturally.  Default is 0 (fringes hidden in @code{default} style).

@item selected-window-accent-custom-color
Custom accent color for the selected window.  When @code{nil}, the mode
uses the current theme's highlight color.  A Pywal color takes
precedence when Pywal is enabled and the selected palette color is
available.

@item selected-window-accent-mode-style
Style for accenting the selected window: @code{default},
@code{tiling}, or @code{subtle}.  Default is @code{default}.

@item selected-window-accent-tiling-outline
When non-nil (the default), @code{tiling} style reserves a header line
on every window so the selected window has an accent on all four sides.
Existing header lines are preserved; a blank header line is added where
needed.  The header line height follows
@code{selected-window-accent-fringe-thickness} and the frame's font
metrics.  The reserved header line reduces each window's text area by
its height.  Set to @code{nil} to omit the top header line.

@item selected-window-accent-percentage-darken
Percentage to darken the theme's highlight color when no custom or
Pywal color is used.  Default is 20.

@item selected-window-accent-percentage-desaturate
Percentage to desaturate the theme's highlight color when no custom or
Pywal color is used.  Default is 20.

@item selected-window-accent-tab-accent
When non-nil, accent the selected tab in the tab bar.

@item selected-window-accent-smart-borders
When non-nil, don't accent single-window frames.  This disables fringe,
header-line, and mode-line accenting when the frame contains only one
window.

@item selected-window-accent-use-pywal
When non-nil and the selected Pywal palette color is available, use a
color from the Pywal palette instead of the custom color or theme
highlight.  The palette is read from
@file{~/.cache/wal/colors.json}.  Default is @code{nil}.

@item selected-window-accent-pywal-color
Which Pywal palette color to use.  The default is @code{"color1"}; the
valid entries are @code{color0} through @code{color15}.
@end table

@section Accessing Customization Interface

You can access all customization options through the Emacs
customization interface:

@example
M-x customize-group RET selected-window-accent RET
@end example

